WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:03.703
TV ratings were huge for the

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:04.437
four day battle

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.205
for House speaker

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:06.006
that resulted in

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.807
Kevin McCarthy's election.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:08.942
Part of the attraction

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.610
was the unfettered access

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.945
given to C-SPAN cameras

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.613
to televise the proceedings

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.749
and provide its feed to outlets

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.250
including CNN.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:17.984
Last week,

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.052
I argued here

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:19.819
that the American

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:20.920
people deserve

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.223
continued unfiltered access

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.724
to the House chamber.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.159
But as soon as Kevin McCarthy

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:27.927
actually took the gavel

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.162
back, went the camera

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.398
to its standard fixed position

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:32.899
aimed at the lectern.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.501
Those rules are set by the party

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:35.769
that controls the House,

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.170
except for special events

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.172
like the election of a House speaker.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.073
Well, Tuesday

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.508
the co-CEO of C-SPAN

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:42.876
sent a letter to McCarthy

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:43.777
seeking permission

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.011
to operate its own

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.113
independent cameras in the chamber,

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.082
citing the positive public reaction

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.084
and the transparency themes

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.420
in McCarthy's own rules package.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:54.721
Among the congressmen

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.256
who have voiced their agreement,

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:58.291
my next guest, Matt Gates,

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.193
you'll remember during the speaker battle

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.327
the camera captured Gates

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:02.962
having private conversations

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:04.697
with a frustrated McCarthy,

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:05.932
chatting with Democrats,

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:08.201
including Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.369
We even saw as

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:10.703
he was almost physically attacked

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:11.404
by fellow

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.939
Congressman Mike Rogers,

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:14.941
who later apologized.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:15.975
So much lies ahead

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.844
for the 118th Congress.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.879
I want these cameras to show it all

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:20.180
be it

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.915
the looming debt limit standoff,

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:22.382
the debates

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.484
over investigations of the FBI

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:26.519
and Hunter Biden, the battles over China

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:28.588
and the origin of COVID 19,

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.256
the blame game of the never ending

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.658
border crisis.

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.692
Florida Congressman Matt

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.027
Gaetz joins me now.

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.262
Congressman, thank you for coming back

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:37.597
without C-SPAN.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:38.331
It occurs to me

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.066
that without C-SPAN cameras

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.201
we would not have seen

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:43.703
you nearly attacked by Mike Rogers.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.305
Do you think that incident

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:46.573
is an example of why

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:47.640
some of your colleagues,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.376
they don't want full camera access?

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.579
No, I think that the reason people

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:53.680
have opposed our effort

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.081
to democratize

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.149
viewing of the House floor

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.251
is because in many of the debates,

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.586
no one's present

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.554
and they want to maintain the fiction

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.256
that that's actual legislating.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:03.957
And whenever

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:06.192
there is sharp disagreement or discord

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.760
or fiery debate on the floor

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:08.561
or in committee

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.797
the camera always catches those moments.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.265
But there are also moments

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.334
of great interpersonal warmth

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.835
and collaboration

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.704
and the forming of alliances

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.272
that you might not normally see.

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.373
Whether it's myself

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:23.343
talking to Ilhan Omar about war powers

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:24.577
or Pramila Jayapal,

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.012
about big tech regulation

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:28.448
or discussing floor strategy with AOC

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:30.316
I think the American people

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.218
would be able to humanize Congress

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.019
more if they saw

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.088
more of our human interactions,

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.689
and not just the theater

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:37.924
that's on display during much

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:38.458
of the debate.

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:41.227
With regard to

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.061
Rogers and

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:42.795
the incident

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.931
which I'm showing now in slo mo.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:47.367
What went on from your perspective?

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.736
How much did you see of him

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.704
coming at you?

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:52.105
Well, Mike

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.439
Rogers and I have worked together

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.340
for six years

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.408
on some of the most

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.976
tense national security issues

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.178
on the Armed Services Committee.

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.846
So it's not the first time

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:01.748
we've been frustrated with one another

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:02.916
or worked together.

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:03.483
Matter of fact,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.752
that wasn't a remarkably unique incident.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:06.819
There are times

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.621
when the pressure cooker is on

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.823
when we do yell and get heated

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:11.891
and that's okay.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:12.825
Among colleagues,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.227
what was different about this

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.129
was just that it was caught on camera.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:17.797
So, you know, Mike and I are fine

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:18.998
we've worked together before.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.733
We will again in the future.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.070
But if we had more dynamic camera angles,

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:26.639
then I think you'd see these flare ups

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:27.273
and then the back

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.242
slapping afterwards in the agreement.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:30.443
And the American people

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.578
could see alliances

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:33.746
form and dissolve in real time

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:34.981
rather than just getting a

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.483
more curated rendition of what happens

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.285
as much as I

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:39.752
want those

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.155
cameras, the camera work in that instance

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:43.323
was a little shoddy

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:44.023
because it followed

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:44.891
Kevin McCarthy

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.593
back into the well of the house,

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:47.894
and we never saw you.

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:49.262
Did you get on your feet?

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.464
Did you get ready for blows?

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.533
I mean, how close did it come?

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:54.100
Oh, no.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.035
I think that it was overblown a bit.

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:56.502
I mean, Mike

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.738
was expressing his frustration with me,

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.406
and I totally understand different

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.108
people had different perspectives

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:03.643
in that moment.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.345
And, you know, the argument

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:06.646
against these cameras

00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:07.347
was actually made

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.881
on your network by Paul Ryan

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.316
this last week when he said, well,

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:11.584
we already have enough

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.453
performance in Congress.

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.387
We don't want people

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.956
playing to the cameras in these moments.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:18.791
I think that the

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.026
public value

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:20.593
of being able

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.595
to see the human interactions

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.764
in frustration and warmth

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.033
and all of those things far outweighs

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:28.167
the risk that people

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.269
will play to the cameras.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:29.569
I mean,

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.371
we have that during debate

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:32.538
one way or the other.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:35.341
I think some of the old guard in Congress

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.376
opposes this

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.443
because they want to continue

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.212
to maintain the fiction

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.346
that when four or five

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:41.614
people are on the floor

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.783
spending millions of dollars,

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.451
that that is actually the action

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.452
of the whole legislator.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.086
Of body

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:48.721
when the reality is far different.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.923
And if we had cameras on the floor,

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:52.959
my suspicion is we would have far better

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:53.793
attendance

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:54.594
during the debates

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:55.595
that impact the lives

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:56.763
of our fellow Americans.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.699
Every Saturday, I have a poll question.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:00.900
As I think you know, during the course

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.568
of the program last Saturday,

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.636
we set a record more

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:07.140
than 46,000 people voted on this issue,

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.542
and it could not have been more decisive.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:10.476
Put up the result of that.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.680
I think it was 90% of people who said,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.715
yeah, 93.91%

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:17.784
they want the camera access today.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.219
Congressman, the Washington Post

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:22.355
lead editorial is on this issue

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:24.557
and agrees with your perspective.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.025
Quick final question. On this.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:28.194
What's the likelihood that you can sway

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.762
Kevin McCarthy?

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.197
Well, I'm encouraged that there's

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:32.932
actually a lot of bipartisan support

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:34.000
for this measure.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.768
Some Democrats have put force

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:36.903
their own proposal.

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:37.603
And so I hope that

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:38.137
if we got

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.972
218 people to agree,

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:41.140
whether they were Republicans

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.208
or Democrats,

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:44.043
that Speaker McCarthy would

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.511
would be very persuaded by that.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.612
And so we're working right now

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.581
to build that bipartisan coalition

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:49.549
to go to the speaker.

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.984
And it is in line with a lot of the goals

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:53.286
the speaker laid out in the Rules

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.254
Committee to have more transparency

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.723
to have more open votes,

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:57.890
to have more open rules.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:58.825
So if we're going to have open

00:05:58.825 --> 00:05:59.392
votes in open

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:01.060
rules, let's have an open chamber

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.662
for all the American people to observe.

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:05.031
On another matter,

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.366
that pertains to video,

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:07.800
you apparently were able

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.802
to earn a concession from him

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.871
that all of the security video

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.473
from Capitol Hill on

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:14.874
January six is going to enter

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:15.908
the public domain.

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:16.576
First of all,

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:17.110
correct me

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:17.910
if I'm wrong,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.145
I believe that was something

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:19.579
that you were able

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:21.047
to get him to agree to.

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.282
Secondly, what is it you want to see?

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.318
What do you expect to see in that video?

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.687
Well, I think that you likely

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:27.954
will see

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:29.455
a lot of exculpatory evidence

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:30.590
where people may have been

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.625
in some sort of technical violation

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:34.060
of federal criminal law,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.095
but never intended to harm anyone

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:36.929
and never intended

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:40.333
to breach any type of security barrier.

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:41.234
A lot of those barriers

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:42.468
may have been taken down.

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:44.637
I'm a believer that transparency answers

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:45.638
a lot of questions

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:46.739
and whether there's

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:47.940
higher criminal acuity

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.842
for some lower criminal acuity

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:50.576
for others.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.245
Releasing that video will be important.

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:52.578
Also,

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.312
we already know

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.680
from whistleblower interviews

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.548
we've done that.

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:57.884
There were federal assets and agents

00:06:57.884 --> 00:06:59.318
that were on the ground that day

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.220
to be able to observe their conduct.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.822
Their potential coordination with one

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:04.424
another would be of great interest

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.092
to many of us on the Judiciary Committee

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.293
and I think many people throughout

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:07.794
the country.

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:10.396
So prosecutors have

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:10.830
said, and

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:11.197
I'm thinking

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:12.398
in the context of the case

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:13.433
of Eric Torrance,

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:14.700
he from Tennessee

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.068
It shouldn't be released

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:16.502
because it's

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:17.937
going to jeopardize security.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:18.971
That too much will enter

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:20.273
the public domain

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:22.175
about the nooks and crannies, my words,

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:23.743
not theirs, of the Capitol.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:24.177
Are you

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:25.778
worried that you're going to jeopardize

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.246
the security of yourself

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:28.114
and your colleagues

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:29.282
if all of this video

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:31.284
comes into the public space?

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:31.617
No.

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.219
Almost every inch of the Capitol

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:35.221
is subject to video surveillance.

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:36.255
If you don't believe that,

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:38.524
go into any casino in America

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.593
and you see the extent to which the zooms

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:42.094
and pans and tilts

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:44.464
inform on how people see these things.

00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:47.266
So if the security of members of Congress

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.168
is dependent on shielding

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:51.237
the perspectives of these cameras,

00:07:51.237 --> 00:07:51.571
that it's

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.339
not a very good security regime,

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:54.841
I think that's a red herring.

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:55.808
And I think it's a

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:56.409
it's an argument

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:57.977
made by the Department of Justice

00:07:57.977 --> 00:07:59.946
because they don't want to expose

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:00.379
the extent

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:00.613
to which

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:02.215
there might have been federal assets

00:08:02.215 --> 00:08:04.383
or agents enhancing criminal acuity.

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:05.384
We don't know that.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:06.953
That's why we want to see the footage.

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:08.321
But I think we'll all be safe

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:09.155
and sound

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.289
even following the release

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:12.024
of that information.

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:13.326
Treasury Secretary Janet

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:14.660
Yellen said that on

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:15.595
I think Thursday,

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:16.963
we're going to hit the debt ceiling

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:18.898
limit of $31 trillion.

00:08:18.898 --> 00:08:20.533
Are we headed for a standoff

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:22.134
where you and your colleagues

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:23.503
are demanding of cuts,

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:24.403
including

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:27.206
Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid?

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.710
We did include in our negotiations

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.513
a reversion to 20, 22 spending levels

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.214
that would require changes in

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:36.148
some of the mandatory

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:37.416
spending, which, of course,

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:38.985
is driven by Medicare,

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:40.353
Medicaid, Social Security.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:42.154
I think work requirements or something

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:42.522
we could get

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:43.890
a lot of bipartisan agreement

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.391
on, particularly in states

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:46.959
that had Medicaid expansion

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:48.027
under Obamacare.

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.062
But remember, just as I teamed up

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:50.796
with Democrats

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:51.864
for the floor action

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:52.732
last week,

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.934
you also may see some Republicans

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.802
in the middle team up with Democrats

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:58.905
to discharge a clean debt limit.

00:08:59.105 --> 00:08:59.739
There's something called

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:00.806
a discharge petition

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:02.008
where you get 218

00:09:02.008 --> 00:09:03.209
signatures on something.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:04.477
It comes right to the floor.

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:06.546
So some of my conservative colleagues

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:07.980
and I may be disappointed

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.482
if you see moderate Republicans

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:10.683
working with Democrats

00:09:10.683 --> 00:09:12.385
to get a clean vote on the debt limit.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:13.886
We would probably like to use

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:15.855
that as an opportunity to analyze

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:17.723
some of the mandatory spending that's

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.559
given us the $32 trillion debt.

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:21.027
You know

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:21.961
that economists say

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:23.462
if we get close to default,

00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:24.730
then what's going to happen?

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.332
Interest rates are going to rise.

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:27.266
People are going to feel it

00:09:27.266 --> 00:09:29.335
in their mortgages, in their auto loans,

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:31.370
in their credit card bills.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:32.772
Yeah, that's why we need to start now.

00:09:32.772 --> 00:09:34.807
We should not engage in brinksmanship.

00:09:34.807 --> 00:09:36.943
We're already having the discussions

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:38.611
with Jody Arrington,

00:09:38.611 --> 00:09:40.212
the new chairman of the Budget Committee,

00:09:40.212 --> 00:09:42.582
about how to create a budget resolution

00:09:42.782 --> 00:09:44.550
to inform on policy choices.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.952
We have an obligation

00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:47.820
as the Republicans in the majority,

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.622
to sell to the American people

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:50.556
work requirements

00:09:50.556 --> 00:09:51.958
or other spending reductions

00:09:51.958 --> 00:09:53.292
to get buy in on that.

00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:54.527
I don't believe we should

00:09:54.527 --> 00:09:55.761
push this to the very end.

00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:56.295
I think we need to

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:57.964
be laying out a plan now

00:09:57.964 --> 00:09:59.532
to get our fiscal house in order.

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:00.666
But as you know, Michael,

00:10:00.666 --> 00:10:02.201
with interest rates rising.

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:04.070
That changes the debt

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.905
service obligations of the country.

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:07.239
And it's one of the major drivers

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.040
of our spending now

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.475
is servicing that debt.

00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:10.343
So we have to be able

00:10:10.343 --> 00:10:11.510
to get it under control.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:13.379
And the math is undeniable

00:10:13.379 --> 00:10:14.947
that as more baby boomers

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:16.048
continue to utilize

00:10:16.048 --> 00:10:16.916
more of the programs

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:18.985
that drive mandatory spending,

00:10:18.985 --> 00:10:20.219
we are going to have that

00:10:20.219 --> 00:10:21.754
take a greater and greater share of

00:10:21.754 --> 00:10:22.555
the federal budget.

